  The 95%% CI half-width IS the smallest effect this corpus could have
  detected for that practice. Read it before reading any point estimate:
  for `docs` (1,190 packages) it is about 4 points, so a real benefit of 6
  points would have shown; for `.Rproj` (22 packages) it is 25 points, so
  that row is uninformative whatever its z said.

  SYNTHETIC POSITIVE CONTROL -- a planted practice with a known benefit.
  Assign a fake practice to a random 1,190 packages (matching `docs`), then
  flip a share of their failing files to successes, and see it recovered.
  Repeated over 20 random assignments, because ONE assignment is itself a
  draw: at a planted benefit of zero the first seed I tried returned -3.5
  points, purely from which packages happened to land in the treated group.
  planted benefit                            mean recovered   mean over 0%        power
  0% of failing files rescued                         +0.2          +0.0           5%
  3% of failing files rescued                         +2.6          +2.4          35%
  6% of failing files rescued                         +5.1          +4.9          75%
  12% of failing files rescued                        +9.9          +9.7         100%
  20% of failing files rescued                       +16.3         +16.1         100%

  'power' is the share of assignments whose bootstrap interval excluded 0.
  At a planted zero it is the false-positive rate and should sit near 5%.
  Read the smallest planted benefit whose power is high: that is the
  smallest real effect this corpus could have shown, and every recommended
  practice tested above is below it or barely at it.

